cm2: a router failure counts once per short stability interval

opensync-lab / opensync-rpi: cm2 restarts OpenSync once an uplink's router check has
failed more than CONFIG_CM2_STABILITY_THRESH_FATAL (8) times in a row, which at the
short stability interval (10 s) is about 80 s without the router. Besides the
periodic check, every Connection_Manager_Uplink change requests a check, and during a
link change those ran back to back, one per 2 s ping timeout: a lab pod whose Multi-AP
uplink EMOSA moved twice in 19 s counted 1 -> 9 in 15 s (07:41:22-07:41:37Z) and
restarted OpenSync ("Restart managers due to exceeding the threshold for fatal
failures"), offline about 3 minutes for a link that was back within seconds.

A failure now counts once per CONFIG_CM2_STABILITY_SHORT_INTERVAL per uplink; checks
in between keep the count and do not repeat its restore actions. A router that stays
unreachable still restarts OpenSync after about 80 s (72 s with checks every 2 s, 80 s
with the periodic check alone); a success still resets the count. The counting is in
cm2_stability_count.h, free of cm2's other state and with the time passed in, so that
pod/opensync/tests/cm2_stability_count_test.c tests it on its own.

Upstream: an OpenSync core behaviour as released in 6.6.1.0:
cm2_connection_req_stability_process() in src/cm2/src/cm2_stability.c counts every
requested check, so the fatal threshold depends on how many checks events request.
Not reported (the owner decides).

